# Chapter 10 Exercises

## 10-1 Using the following table definitions and data, write a query that returns each cus
tomer name along with their total payments. Include all customers, even if no payment records exist for that customer
### Solution
mysql> SELECT CONCAT(c.first_name, ' ', c.last_name) customer_name, SUM(p.amount) total_payments
    -> FROM customer c
    -> LEFT OUTER JOIN payment p
    -> ON c.customer_id = p.customer_id
    -> GROUP BY c.customer_id;
+-----------------------+----------------+
| customer_name         | total_payments |
+-----------------------+----------------+
| MARY SMITH            |         118.68 |
| PATRICIA JOHNSON      |         128.73 |
| LINDA WILLIAMS        |         135.74 |
...
| WADE DELVALLE         |          83.78 |
| AUSTIN CINTRON        |          83.81 |
+-----------------------+----------------+
599 rows in set (0.17 sec)

## 10-2 Reformulate your query from Exercise 10-1 to use the other outer join type (e.g., if
you used a left outer join in Exercise 10-1, use a right outer join this time) such that
the results are identical to Exercise 10-1.
### Solution
  mysql> SELECT CONCAT(c.first_name, ' ', c.last_name) customer_name, SUM(p.amount) total_payments
    -> FROM payment p
    -> RIGHT OUTER JOIN customer c
    -> ON p.customer_id = c.customer_id
    -> GROUP BY c.customer_id;
+-----------------------+----------------+
| customer_name         | total_payments |
+-----------------------+----------------+
| MARY SMITH            |         118.68 |
